{"name":"Moose Trail","hex":"#6b5445","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The largest member of the deer family (Alces americanus, sometimes included in Alces alces), of which the male has very large, palmate antlers.","Any of the extinct moose-like deer of the genera Cervalces and Libralces."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Asian girl taken as a lover."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moose"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moose","blocks":[{"text":"The moose or elk is the world's tallest, largest and heaviest extant species of deer and the only extant species in the genus Alces. It is also the tallest, and the second-largest, land animal in North America, falling short only to the American bison in body mass. Most adult male moose have broad, palmate antlers; most other members of the deer family have pointed antlers with a dendritic (\"twig-like\") configuration. Moose inhabit the circumpolar boreal forests or temperate broadleaf and mixed forests of the Northern Hemisphere, thriving in cooler, temperate areas as well as subarctic climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Alaska_moose.jpg/330px-Alaska_moose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moose","wikidata":"Q35517"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Alce_%28Alces_alces%29%2C_Potter_marsh%2C_Alaska%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2017-08-22%2C_DD_139.jpg/500px-Alce_%28Alces_alces%29%2C_Potter_marsh%2C_Alaska%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2017-08-22%2C_DD_139.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Alce_%28Alces_alces%29%2C_Potter_marsh%2C_Alaska%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2017-08-22%2C_DD_139.jpg/1280px-Alce_%28Alces_alces%29%2C_Potter_marsh%2C_Alaska%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2017-08-22%2C_DD_139.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Alce_%28Alces_alces%29%2C_Potter_marsh%2C_Alaska%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2017-08-22%2C_DD_139.jpg/500px-Alce_%28Alces_alces%29%2C_Potter_marsh%2C_Alaska%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2017-08-22%2C_DD_139.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fil